  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY NIGHT THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 212 PM CDT Thu Aug 27 2026\n\nThe weekend and into next week are looking to remain dry, as\nupper level subsidence sets in as the ridge moves east over into\nthe central CONUS. The exception is Sunday and Monday, where\nnortheastern MO and west-central IL could see some rainfall.\nModel guidance indicates strong warm air advection on the nose\nof the low level jet over IA and western Great Lakes will\nprovide support for multiple rounds of convection in that area.\nConvective remnants on the southern edge of these systems could\ndip south into northeastern MO and west-central IL, though\nconfidence in this happening is low. Currently, LREF\nprobabilities of even measurable precipitation are 20-40%, while\nthe probabilities for even somewhat meaningful rainfall (>0.25\u201d)\nare even lower at less than 10%.\n\nAs the ridge sets in, higher temperatures are expected to be\nwell above average beginning Sunday. Cluster analysis shows that\nthe 500 mb heights associated with the ridge are anomalously\nhigh, being above the 95th percentile compared to climatology.\nCurrent ensemble guidance is in agreement that once this\nunseasonal ridge sets in over the central CONUS, it will remain\nover the region keeping the above average temperatures around\nfor most, if not all, of the week. The 850 mb temperatures will\nbe at least in the low to mid 20s C (above 95th climatological\npercentile), translating to surface temperatures in the mid to\nupper 90s, There is a chance (30-50%) that central and east-\ncentral MO may reach 100 F or higher, which could be caused by\nsouthwesterly downsloping flow off the Ozark Plateau. While the\ntemperatures are expected to be above average and perhaps the\nwarmest we\u2019ve seen all year, the dewpoints are anticipated to be\nnotably lower compared to previous heatwaves. Model guidance\nindicates dewpoints in the low to mid 60s, with a small chance\n(20-40%) of dewpoints 70 F or greater in northeastern MO and\nwest-central IL on Tuesday and Wednesday. As a result, heat\nindex values in the upper 90s are possible with some areas\npeaking to near 100 F by mid week. This means that despite\npotentially warmer temperatures than earlier in the year, this\nheatwave is not expected to be as extreme as those from earlier\nin the summer.",
